[ADD] populate: New `populate` module for synthetic data generation

Proper evaluation of performance patches requires databases with realistic,
high-volume data. Demo data lacks the volume, and the existing populate
tool (merged in v18) copies from the initial dataset, producing skewed
distributions that lead to incorrect conclusions.

The old populate framework also had structural problems: it populated entire
model dependency graphs whether needed or not, ran single-core, required
hooks on BaseModel, and gave developers too much unconstrained freedom,
resulting in poorly optimized scripts that were hard to maintain.

This new framework takes a declarative, data-driven approach (Blueprints)
to address those shortcomings:
* Blueprints target only the models relevant to what's being benchmarked,
  avoiding unnecessary population of unrelated models.
* A declarative structure prevents poorly written generation scripts and
  allows the framework to parallelize work transparently via `-j <N>` or
  `-j auto`, without developers needing an explicit parallel API.
* A rich generator suite (scalar, textual, temporal, choice, relational,
  reference, faker, misc, and properties) with statistical distributions
  (normal, uniform, exponential, beta, poisson, triangular) produces
  plausible, realistic data rather than uniformly distributed noise.
* Virtual fields for intermediate computations, write jobs to update
  previously created records, and blueprint inheritance allows expressive
  scenarios without writing elaborate Python code.
* Generated records carry external references (like xmlids), making them
  traceable and referenceable across blueprints.
* Session tracking with resume support after interruption (`--resume`).
* Low maintenance overhead: adding or removing a field is typically a
  one-line XML change, and new blueprints can be bootstrapped quickly.

The end goal is to make [PERF] PR benchmarking accessible to all
developers: a reviewer should be able to reproduce a realistic test
database with a single command.

